Ingredients
4 cups (500g) all-purpose flour or bread flour

1 ½ tsp (9g) fine salt

½ tsp (2g) active dry yeast or instant yeast

1 ½ cups (360ml) lukewarm water (around 100°F / 38°C)

Step-by-Step Instructions
Mix the Dough:
In a large bowl, whisk together the flour, salt, and yeast. Add the lukewarm water and stir with a wooden spoon or spatula until a shaggy dough forms and no dry flour remains.

First Rise (12–18 Hours):
Cover the bowl tightly with plastic wrap or a damp towel. Let it sit at room temperature for 12 to 18 hours (overnight works great). The dough will double in size, become bubbly, and look very soft.

Preheat the Oven & Dutch Oven:
Place a 4- to 6-quart Dutch oven (with the lid on) inside your oven. Preheat the oven to 450°F (230°C) for 30 minutes so the pot gets piping hot.

Shape the Dough:
Lightly flour a sheet of parchment paper. Scrape the sticky dough onto the paper. Gently fold the edges into the center to form a rough round ball. Cover loosely with a towel while the oven finishes preheating (about 15–20 minutes).

Bake Covered:
Carefully remove the hot Dutch oven from the oven and remove the lid. Pick up the dough using the corners of the parchment paper and drop it (parchment and all) into the pot. Put the lid back on and bake for 30 minutes.

Bake Uncovered:
Remove the lid and bake for another 15 to 20 minutes, until the crust is deep golden-brown and crisp.

Cool:
Transfer the bread to a wire rack. Let it cool for at least 30 minutes before slicing so the interior crumb finishes setting.

Tips for Success
High Heat is Key: The covered Dutch oven traps steam from the dough, giving you a thin, crispy crust and great oven spring.

Instant Yeast Alternative: If using instant yeast, you can shorten the rise time to 2 to 3 hours in a warm spot, though a longer rise yields a richer flavor.